speechtext:
Oh, charmed with it;
they must be very fond of it. A reduction has been made of a few paltry cents upon ploughs and upon a great many other articles that apparently we obtain only from Great Britain. Just imagine bringing a farm cart from Great Britain-and you get 'a reduction of 2-1 per cent on that article; but you cannot get any reduction if you bring it from across the border. Now, who in thunder among the farmers expects to get any benefit out of that part of the policy? Of course, my hon. friends to my left mildly applaud it and say it is the best thing that could be done under the circumstances-a "step in the right direction." Well, when they have stepped out on the prairies it will be a case of "Tell it not in Gath; publish it not in the streets of Askelon." That is the way this news will be received if my hon. friends go fresh from Parliament with that kind of thing going on.
Then, let us look at these proposals for a moment from the standpoint of the labourer. I notice that the labourer gets the benefit of the 21 per cent reduction on his heavy knitted wool undershirt, while the man who is fortunate enough to be able to wear collars and cuffs to his work, gets, I think, a reduction of 10 per cent, or 5- it is twice as much, anyway.
